---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="About | Category Checker" description="What Category Checker is, who it's for, and how the provider architecture works.">
  <section class="wrap flex max-w-[720px] flex-col gap-6 py-16">
    <h1 class="text-[36px] font-medium leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.5px] text-heading">About Category Checker</h1>

    <p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-fg">
      Category Checker is a free MSNugget tool that looks up how a website or URL is categorized by Microsoft Global
      Secure Access web content filtering, part of Microsoft Entra Internet Access. It's built for Global Secure
      Access administrators, security administrators, network administrators, cloud architects, and IT
      professionals who need to confirm a category-based access decision quickly, without opening the full Entra
      admin center.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-4 text-[20px] font-medium text-heading">Why it exists</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-fg">
      Category-based access policies only work correctly if the category you expect for a domain is the category
      the enforcement point actually uses. Global Secure Access web content filtering policies are written against
      categories like "Social Media," "Streaming Media," or "Business" — get the category wrong (or assume a domain
      is categorized when it isn't) and you either block something you meant to allow, or leave something reachable
      that a policy was supposed to catch. Category Checker exists to make that one lookup fast, so it's a two-second
      check instead of a policy-testing round trip.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-4 text-[20px] font-medium text-heading">How it works</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-fg">
      Category Checker calls the Microsoft Graph <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-accent">getWebCategoryByUrl</code>{" "}
      API — the same categorization data Global Secure Access itself enforces against — using an app-only Entra ID
      registration with the <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-accent">NetworkAccess.Read.All</code> permission.
      Every provider (Microsoft Global Secure Access today, more planned) implements the same{" "}
      <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-accent">CategoryProvider</code> interface, so adding another vendor's
      real API later doesn't change how results are displayed. See{" "}
      <a href="https://msnugget.com/" class="underline hover:text-heading">msnugget.com</a> for the rest of the MSNugget
      tool family, and this project's <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-accent">ARCHITECTURE.md</code> for the technical
      design.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-4 text-[20px] font-medium text-heading">Why aren't FortiGuard, Cisco Umbrella, Zscaler, and Netskope wired up yet?</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-fg">
      Each of those vendors either requires a paid subscription and API credentials we don't have, or only offers a
      free public lookup page that's actively protected against exactly the kind of automated request this tool
      would need to make. Rather than scrape around that protection — which would be fragile and against the
      vendor's intent — Category Checker only shows real results for providers it has legitimate API access to.
      They'll be added as real integrations if and when that access is available.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-4 text-[20px] font-medium text-heading">Independence</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-fg">
      Category Checker is an independent, community-built tool. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by
      Microsoft, Fortinet, Cisco, Zscaler, or Netskope.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
